    //- ── Getting Started ──────────────────
    .help-section
      button.help-section-title(type='button', (click)='toggleHelpSection("getting-started")')
        span.help-chevron {{ isSectionOpen('getting-started') ? '▾' : '▸' }}
        | Getting Started & Shortcuts
      .help-section-body(*ngIf='isSectionOpen("getting-started")')
        p
          strong Adding devices:
          |  Drag a device type (Router, Switch, Firewall, Server, Cloud, PC, Host Port, Bridge) from the left palette onto the canvas. Click a node to select it and edit properties in the right panel.
        p
          strong Creating links:
          |  Press
          |
          span.help-kbd L
          |  to enter Link Mode, then click source → target node. Or drag from one node to another. A port picker appears to select specific interfaces.
        p
          strong Canvas navigation:
          |  Drag the canvas background to pan. Mouse wheel to zoom. Use Fit / Reset View (View menu or toolbar) to re-center.
        p
          strong Toolbar:
          |  The top bar includes quick-access buttons — Save (↓), Undo (↺), Redo (↻), Link Mode (⤢), Fit View (⊕), Theme Toggle (☀/☾), Start All (green dot), Stop All (gray dot). Buttons disable when not applicable.
        p
          strong Selection:
          |  Click a node or link to select. Shift+Click or Ctrl+Click to add/remove from multi-selection.
        p
          strong Annotations:
          |  Double-click empty canvas space to create a text annotation. Double-click to edit, drag to move, click ✕ to delete.
        p
          strong Topology description:
          |  Click the description area in the top bar (next to the topology name) to add or edit a text description for the current topology.
        p
          strong Search:
          |  Press
          |
          span.help-kbd Ctrl+F
          |  to search nodes by name, type, or management IP. Click a result to center the viewport on that node.
        p
          strong Right-click:
          |  Node → Start / Stop / Suspend / Add Link / Rename / SSH Terminal / Delete. Link → Mark Link Down (toggles up/down) / Properties / Reset Bend / Delete.
        table.help-table
          thead: tr
            th Shortcut
            th Action
          tbody
            tr
              td: span.help-kbd Ctrl+S / Ctrl+O
              td Save / Open topology
            tr
              td: span.help-kbd Ctrl+Shift+S
              td Save workspace (all tabs)
            tr
              td: span.help-kbd Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Shift+Z
              td Undo / Redo (Ctrl+Y also redoes)
            tr
              td: span.help-kbd Ctrl+C / Ctrl+V
              td Copy / Paste nodes
            tr
              td: span.help-kbd Ctrl+A
              td Select all
            tr
              td: span.help-kbd Ctrl+F
              td Search nodes
            tr
              td: span.help-kbd L
              td Toggle Link Mode
            tr
              td: span.help-kbd Delete
              td Delete selected items
            tr
              td: span.help-kbd Escape
              td Cancel / close dialogs
            tr
              td: span.help-kbd F1
              td Toggle this Help window

    //- ── Templates & Build Custom ─────────
    .help-section
      button.help-section-title(type='button', (click)='toggleHelpSection("templates")')
        span.help-chevron {{ isSectionOpen('templates') ? '▾' : '▸' }}
        | Templates & Build Custom
      .help-section-body(*ngIf='isSectionOpen("templates")')
        p
          strong Templates
          |  (File → Templates): 121 preconfigured topologies across multiple categories. Click a card to load it (replaces the current topology). Filter by category:
        ul
          li
            span.help-tag General
            |  — Point-to-Point, Hub-Spoke, Ring, Full Mesh, 3-Tier
          li
            span.help-tag Datacenter
            |  — Spine-Leaf, Clos, Dual-ToR, K8s Fabric, HA Cluster, Primary + DR
          li
            span.help-tag Enterprise
            |  — Campus, Branch Office, SD-WAN
          li
            span.help-tag Service Provider
            |  — MPLS Core, Metro Ring, Mobile Backhaul
          li
            span.help-tag DCI
            |  — Back-to-Back, Over WAN, EVPN Multi-site
          li
            span.help-tag Security
            |  — DMZ, Dual Firewall
          li
            span.help-tag WAN
            |  — Hub-Spoke, Dual ISP Failover
          li
            span.help-tag IPv6 / Segment Routing / Routing
            |  — Protocol-focused topologies (BGP, OSPF, IS-IS, SR-MPLS, SRv6)
          li
            span.help-tag Juniper / SONiC / AI-ML
            |  — Vendor-specific templates with preconfigured models, VLANs, and protocols
        p Templates with vendor nodes include a "Download Config" button to export startup configurations.

        p
          strong Build Custom
          |  (File → Build Custom): A 4-step wizard to design a custom topology from scratch.
        p
          strong Step 1 — Nodes:
          |  Add role groups (spine, leaf, border-leaf, super-spine, tor, gateway, core, aggregation, access, custom). Set count (1–64), vendor, model, speed (10M–800G), and port count (2–128) per group.
        p
          strong Step 2 — Links:
          |  Define connectivity rules between roles. Patterns:
        ul
          li
            span.help-tag Full-Mesh
            |  — every source connects to every target
          li
            span.help-tag Ring
            |  — circular topology
          li
            span.help-tag Dual-Homed
            |  — each target connects to ≥2 sources for redundancy
        p Set links per pair (1, 2, or 4) for LAG/ECMP.
        p
          strong Step 3 — Addressing:
          |  Topology name, IP mode (IPv4 / IPv6 / Dual Stack), base CIDRs for link and loopback subnets. Select underlay protocol:
        ul
          li
            span.help-tag eBGP Clos
            |  — unique ASN per node (configurable spine/leaf ASN start)
          li
            span.help-tag iBGP with Route Reflector
            |  — shared ASN, spines as reflectors
          li
            span.help-tag OSPF / OSPFv3 / IS-IS
            |  — area and level auto-assigned
        p Optional EVPN-VXLAN overlay (VNI base configurable). Service profile and VLAN template selection. Toggle auto-assign IPs and generate vendor startup configs.
        p
          strong Step 4 — Preview:
          |  Interactive canvas showing node layout by tier, links, and service profile details. Click "Confirm Build" to create the topology (replaces current).

    //- ── Topology Design ──────────────────
    .help-section
      button.help-section-title(type='button', (click)='toggleHelpSection("topology")')
        span.help-chevron {{ isSectionOpen('topology') ? '▾' : '▸' }}
        | Topology Design
      .help-section-body(*ngIf='isSectionOpen("topology")')
        p
          strong Node types:
          |  Router, Switch, Firewall, Server, Cloud, PC, Host Port (teal — maps to a physical NIC on the server), Bridge (purple — maps to a Linux/libvirt/OVS bridge).
        p
          strong Node properties panel
          |  — click a node to open the right panel with 7 tabs:
        ul
          li
            span.help-tag Info
            |  — Name, description, vendor, model, serial, ASN, OSPF area, IS-IS level, SR-MPLS SID, SRv6 locator, MPLS LDP, management IP, SSH credentials, node role, RAM, image/template, port count, source/asset ID. Vendor-specific options (e.g. Juniper switch type, QFX/EX model).
          li
            span.help-tag Ports
            |  — IP address (CIDR), description, speed, VLAN mode (access/trunk), VLAN ID, trunk allowed VLANs, trunk native VLAN, enable/disable per port, breakout/channelization. Front-panel diagram: green = linked, gray = free, dark = disabled.
          li
            span.help-tag VLANs
            |  — Create and manage VLAN entries for the node. Badge shows VLAN count.
          li
            span.help-tag Config
            |  — View and edit the startup configuration. Action buttons:
            |
            span.help-code Apply
            |  (save edits) ·
            span.help-code Clear
            |  ·
            span.help-code Copy
            |  ·
            span.help-code Download
            |  (save as .cfg file) ·
            span.help-code Expand
            |  (open in a full-size editor window with Ctrl+S to save back) ·
            span.help-code Snippets
            |  (open the Config Snippet Library) ·
            span.help-code Push to Device
            |  (SSH push to physical device or container — requires mgmt IP + SSH credentials and vendor set).
          li
            span.help-tag Notes
            |  — Free-text notes for the node (operational comments, change history, etc.).
          li
            span.help-tag Links
            |  — Lists all peer connections for this node. Badge shows link count.
          li
            span.help-tag Inventory
            |  — Polled data (OS version, model, uptime). Poll Sync: review and selectively apply polled changes. Config Backups: view history, compare any two versions with inline diff. Shows alarm badge when the node has active alarms.
        p
          strong Links:
          |  Type (Ethernet/Serial), bandwidth (1G–100G), latency, packet loss, duplex. Drag midpoint to curve; right-click → Reset Bend to straighten.
        p
          strong Auto-assign IPs
          |  (Devices menu): /30 IPv4, loopbacks, /127 IPv6, ULA loopbacks. Dialog offers CIDR base and "overwrite existing" toggle.
        p
          strong IPAM Panel
          |  (View menu): Table of all assigned IPs sorted by address — Node / Port / IP.
        p
          strong Config generation:
          |  Vendor-specific startup configs (Cisco, Junos, Arista, Huawei, Nokia, SONiC, HPE, Dell, MikroTik, Extreme). Protocols: OSPF, BGP, IS-IS, SR-MPLS, SRv6, MPLS LDP, EVPN-VXLAN.
        p
          strong Service profiles
          |  (Devices → Apply Service Profile): Preconfigured templates with overwrite and regenerate options.
        p
          strong Visualization modes
          |  (View menu):
        ul
          li
            span.help-tag VLAN View
            |  — blue = trunk, green = access, gray = none. Nodes show VLAN count badge.
          li
            span.help-tag BGP View
            |  — orange = eBGP, purple = iBGP, gray = no ASN. Nodes show ASN badge.
          li
            span.help-tag Default (Link Quality)
            |  — When neither VLAN nor BGP view is active, links color by status: blue = good, orange = degraded, red = down.
        p
          strong Dark / Light Theme:
          |  Click the ☀ / ☾ button in the toolbar (next to Fit View) to toggle between dark and light themes. Your preference is saved to localStorage and persisted across sessions. The app also respects the system
          |
          span.help-code prefers-color-scheme
          |  on first launch.
        p
          strong View toggles
          |  (View menu):
          |
          span.help-tag Show IP Labels
          |  ·
          span.help-tag Show Interface Labels
          |  — overlay port IPs or interface names on links.
          |
          span.help-tag IPv6 Labels
          |  — show IPv6 addresses on links (in addition to IPv4).
          |
          span.help-tag Straight Links
          |  — switch between curved and straight link rendering.
        p
          strong Canvas View Bar:
          |  A floating toolbar at the bottom center of the canvas provides quick one-click toggles for the most used view options:
          strong  IP
          |  ·
          strong  Intf
          |  ·
          strong  IPv6
          |  ·
          strong  VLAN
          |  ·
          strong  BGP
          |  ·
          strong  Flows
          |  ·
          strong  Routes
          |  ·
          strong  Counters
          |  ·
          strong  Map
          | . Active views are highlighted. VLAN, BGP, and Flows are mutually exclusive. The bar only appears when the topology has nodes.
        p
          strong Auto-Layout
          |  (View menu) — four layout algorithms:
        ul
          li
            span.help-tag Force-Directed
            |  — Fruchterman-Reingold physics simulation. Nodes repel, links attract. Good for general-purpose organic layouts.
          li
            span.help-tag Hierarchical
            |  — Sugiyama-style layered layout. Multi-source BFS assigns nodes to layers by role (super-spine → spine → leaf → access). Barycenter heuristic minimizes link crossings. Best for datacenter spine-leaf fabrics.
          li
            span.help-tag Radial
            |  — Places the highest-degree node at center, BFS rings radiate outward. Ideal for hub-spoke or star topologies.
          li
            span.help-tag Grid
            |  — Sorts nodes by type and label, arranges in a uniform grid. Useful for inventory-style views.
        p
          strong Traffic Flow Visualization
          |  (View menu →
          |
          span.help-tag Traffic Flows
          | ):
        ul
          li Toggle
            |
            span.help-tag Traffic Flow View
            |  to color links by defined flows (animated dashed paths show direction).
          li Open the
            |
            span.help-tag Traffic Flows Panel
            |  (side panel) to add, edit, or remove flows.
          li Each flow has: name, source node, destination node, path (list of links), color, and bandwidth.
          li Flows render as animated dash overlays on top of regular link lines.
        p
          strong Interface Counters
          |  (View menu →
          |
          span.help-tag Interface Counters
          | ): Opens a side panel showing real-time interface counter data (packets in/out, errors, discards) for all polled devices. Counters are fetched via SSH using vendor-specific commands. Live polling refreshes automatically.
        p
          strong Validate Topology
          |  (Export menu): Checks for isolated nodes (no links), duplicate port IPs, duplicate management IPs, and empty topology. Results shown in the status bar.
        p
          strong Simulate
          |  (Simulate menu or right-click): Start / Suspend / Stop nodes. Suspend pauses the node without fully stopping it — useful for simulating maintenance windows. Status dots: green = running, yellow = suspended, gray = stopped.
        p
          strong Canvas indicators:
          |  "M" badge = device mapped via CSV import. Red badge = node has critical alarms. VLAN count badge appears in VLAN View. ASN badge appears in BGP View.

    //- ── Containerlab ───────────────────────
    .help-section
      button.help-section-title(type='button', (click)='toggleHelpSection("containerlab")')
        span.help-chevron {{ isSectionOpen('containerlab') ? '▾' : '▸' }}
        | Containerlab
      .help-section-body(*ngIf='isSectionOpen("containerlab")')
        h3 Lab Deployment
        p
          strong Deploy:
          |  Simulate menu → Deploy Containerlab, or Export menu → Containerlab Topology → Deploy button. Checks Docker and containerlab CLI prerequisites, saves .clab.yml to disk (default ~/containerlab-labs/), then opens a terminal with
          code  sudo clab deploy
          | .
        p
          strong Destroy:
          |  Simulate menu → Destroy Lab. Opens a terminal with
          code  sudo clab destroy
          |  to tear down all containers and virtual links.
        p
          strong Inspect:
          |  Simulate menu → Inspect Lab Status. Runs
          code  clab inspect
          |  to show container states, management IPs, and kinds. Automatically updates node status on the canvas.
        p
          strong Prerequisites:
          |  Docker daemon running and containerlab CLI installed (https://containerlab.dev). Deploy/destroy require sudo.

        h3 Installing Prerequisites
        p Two components are required before you can deploy a lab: Docker and containerlab.
        h4 1. Install Docker
        p
          strong macOS:
        ul
          li Install Docker Desktop via Homebrew:
          .help-example
            span.help-code brew install --cask docker
          li Or download from
            |
            span.help-code  https://www.docker.com/products/docker-desktop
          li After install, launch Docker Desktop from Applications — wait for the whale icon in the menu bar to show "Docker Desktop is running"
          li Docker Desktop must be running before deploying a lab
        p
          strong Linux (Ubuntu / Debian / CentOS):
        ul
          li Install Docker Engine via the convenience script:
          .help-example
            span.help-code curl -fsSL https://get.docker.com | sh
          li Add your user to the docker group (avoids sudo for docker commands):
          .help-example
            span.help-code sudo usermod -aG docker $USER
          li Log out and back in for group changes to take effect
          li Start and enable Docker:
          .help-example
            span.help-code sudo systemctl start docker && sudo systemctl enable docker
        p
          strong Verify Docker:
          |  Run
          |
          span.help-code  docker info
          |  — the Deploy dialog also checks this automatically (✓ green = running)

        h4 2. Install Containerlab
        p
          strong Linux (recommended — native binary):
        ul
          li One-line install:
          .help-example
            span.help-code bash -c "$(curl -sL https://get.containerlab.dev)"
          li This installs the
            |
            span.help-code  containerlab
            |  binary to
            |
            span.help-code  /usr/bin/containerlab
          li Verify:
            |
            span.help-code  containerlab version
        p
          strong macOS (Docker wrapper — no native binary):
        ul
          li Containerlab does not install natively on macOS. Use the official Docker container wrapper instead.
          li Add these aliases to your
            |
            span.help-code  ~/.zshrc
            | :
          .help-example
            span.help-code alias containerlab='docker run --rm -it --privileged --network host -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock -v /var/run/netns:/var/run/netns -v $(pwd):/lab --pid host ghcr.io/srl-labs/clab containerlab'
            br
            span.help-code alias clab='containerlab'
          li Reload your shell:
            |
            span.help-code  source ~/.zshrc
          li Verify:
            |
            span.help-code  containerlab version
          li The app auto-detects the Docker wrapper — no additional configuration needed
        p
          strong Auto-install from the app:
          |  If containerlab is not found, the Deploy dialog shows a ✗ red status with an "Install" button. Clicking it runs the install script automatically.
        p
          strong Deploy/destroy require sudo:
          |  The terminal window opened by the app will prompt for your password. On macOS with the Docker wrapper, sudo is also needed for container network namespace operations.

        p
          strong SSH to virtual nodes:
          |  After deploy, use Inspect Lab Status to see management IPs. Then right-click a node → Open SSH Terminal (default SONiC-VS credentials: admin / YourPaSsWoRd).

        h3 User Workflow
        p The end-to-end workflow for building, deploying, and managing a containerlab topology:
        p
          strong Step 1 — Design Topology:
        ul
          li Drag-and-drop Router, Switch, or Host nodes onto the canvas
          li Set
            |
            span.help-code Vendor
            |  (Juniper, SONiC, Arista, Nokia, etc.) and optionally
            |
            span.help-code Device Type
            |  /
            |
            span.help-code Model
            |  in node properties
          li The 🐳 containerlab kind hint below the model field shows the mapped kind, Docker image, and boot time
          li Connect nodes with links — each link auto-assigns interface pairs
        p
          strong Step 2 — Assign IPs:
        ul
          li Use
            |
            span.help-code Design → Auto-address Links
            |  to assign /30 pairs from a base CIDR (e.g. 10.20.0.0/16)
          li Or manually set IPs on individual interfaces via link properties
          li Optionally set a management subnet in the deploy dialog
        p
          strong Step 3 — Choose Server:
        ul
          li Open
            |
            span.help-code Simulate → Deploy Containerlab
          li The
            strong  Server
            |  selector at the top defaults to
            strong  Local
          li To deploy on a remote Linux host, click
            strong  Manage
            |  → "+ Add Server" → enter SSH details → Save
          li Select the desired server from the dropdown — prereqs re-check automatically
        p
          strong Step 4 — Check Prerequisites & Images:
        ul
          li The dialog verifies Docker and containerlab on the active server (local or remote)
          li Fix any issues: Start Docker, Install containerlab
          li
            strong Docker Images
            |  section shows required images with status:
          li ✓ green = available and correct architecture
          li ⚠ amber = wrong architecture (e.g. amd64 on Apple Silicon) — Deploy is blocked
          li ✗ red = not found locally
          li To load an image: click
            strong  Import Image File
            |  → select your
            span.help-code  .tgz
            |  /
            span.help-code  .tar
            |  file — the app auto-fixes Juniper symlink issues and auto-tags to the expected name (e.g.
            span.help-code  crpd:latest
            | )
          li Or click
            strong  Pull
            |  to download from Docker Hub / registry
          li For remote: ensure SSH agent has your key loaded (
            span.help-code ssh-add
            | )
        p
          strong Step 5 — Select Docker Image:
        ul
          li The
            strong  Docker image for SONiC-VS nodes
            |  dropdown lets you pick from available local Docker images
          li The default is
            code  netreplica/docker-sonic-vs:latest
            |  but you can select any compatible image (e.g. a custom build like
            code  docker-sonic-vs:latest
            | )
          li Images with architecture mismatches (e.g. amd64 on Apple Silicon) show a warning but can still run via Docker Desktop's Rosetta emulation
        p
          strong Step 6 — Deploy:
        ul
          li Click
            strong  Deploy
            |  — the app generates
            |
            span.help-code .clab.yml
            |  plus any startup configs
          li
            strong Local:
            |  saves files to disk, opens terminal with
            |
            span.help-code sudo clab deploy
          li
            strong Remote:
            |  uploads files via SFTP, opens terminal with
            |
            span.help-code ssh -t user@host 'sudo clab deploy ...'
          li A
            strong  progress banner
            |  appears centered on the canvas showing deployment status with a countdown timer until container inspection
          li Wait for containers to boot (crpd ~30s, SONiC-VS ~3min, vrnetlab VMs ~7-15min)
        p
          strong Step 7 — Inspect & Monitor:
        ul
          li
            span.help-code Simulate → Inspect Lab Status
            |  — shows container states, management IPs, updates canvas node status
          li
            span.help-code Simulate → Start Live Monitor
            |  — polls container health + BGP neighbor states every 10s
          li
            span.help-code Simulate → Detect Running Lab
            |  — finds existing labs to reconnect or import
        p
          strong Step 8 — Interact with Nodes:
        ul
          li
            strong Open Console:
            |  right-click node → Open Console — opens vendor CLI (vtysh, cli, sr_cli, Cli, sh)
          li
            strong Fetch Config:
            |  right-click node → Fetch Config — retrieves running config
          li
            strong Push Config:
            |  right-click node → Push Config — pushes config commands into the container
          li For remote servers, console opens via
            |
            span.help-code ssh -t user@host 'docker exec -it ...'
        p
          strong Step 9 — Destroy:
        ul
          li
            span.help-code Simulate → Destroy Lab
            |  — tears down all containers and virtual links
          li For remote:
            |
            span.help-code ssh -t user@host 'sudo clab destroy ...'

        h3 Remote Server Management
        p
          | By default, all containerlab operations run on the
          strong  Local
          |  machine. You can add remote Linux servers to deploy labs on dedicated hardware with more resources or KVM support (required for vrnetlab VMs). The remote server workflow is modelled after GNS3-style server management.

        h4 Step 1 — Open Server Manager
        p Open
          strong  Simulate → Server Manager…
          |  from the menu, or click the server indicator in the toolbar (shows current server name with a colored status dot).

        h4 Step 2 — Add a Remote Server
        p Click
          strong  + Add Server
          |  and fill in the connection details:
        table.help-table
          thead: tr
            th Field
            th Description
          tbody
            tr
              td Name
              td Friendly label, e.g. "Lab Server 01"
            tr
              td SSH Host
              td IP address or hostname of the remote Linux server
            tr
              td SSH Username
              td Login user on the remote server (e.g.
                code  admin
                | )
            tr
              td SSH Password
              td Enter password for password-based auth. Leave empty to use SSH key via ssh-agent
            tr
              td SSH Port
              td Default
                code  22
            tr
              td Remote Lab Directory
              td Where topology files are uploaded (default
                code  /tmp/containerlab-labs
                | )
        p Click
          strong  Save
          |  to store the server profile.

        h4 Step 3 — Authentication
        p Two authentication methods are supported:
        ul
          li
            strong Password auth:
            |  Enter the SSH password in the server profile. The password is used for all SSH, SFTP, and Docker SSH transport operations.
          li
            strong SSH key auth (ssh-agent):
            |  Leave the password field empty. Ensure your key is loaded:
            code  ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_rsa
        p If both password and ssh-agent are available, password takes priority.

        h4 Step 4 — Test the Connection
        p In the Server Manager table, click
          strong  Test
          |  next to your server. The test verifies three things:
        ul
          li
            strong SSH connectivity
            |  — Can the app connect to the remote host?
          li
            strong Docker
            |  — Is Docker Engine running on the remote server?
          li
            strong Containerlab
            |  — Is containerlab available (binary or Docker image)?
        p All three checks must pass before you can deploy labs. Fix any issues on the remote host first (install Docker, install containerlab).

        h4 Step 5 — Server Connectivity
        p All servers are automatically tested and connected when added. The Server Manager shows live status (CPU, memory, disk, Docker, KVM) for each server. You can select which server to deploy to in the Deploy dialog's
          strong  Compute Host
          |  dropdown.

        h4 Step 6 — Build & Deploy
        ol
          li Design your topology on the canvas as usual — drag nodes, create links, configure properties
          li Open
            strong  Simulate → Deploy Containerlab
          li The app will:
        ul
          li Generate the
            code  .clab.yml
            |  topology file from your canvas
          li Upload the YAML and any startup configs to the remote server via SFTP
          li Run
            code  sudo clab deploy
            |  on the remote server
          li Show deploy output in the terminal panel

        h4 Step 7 — Monitor & Interact
        ul
          li The
            strong  toolbar indicator
            |  shows real-time connection status — green (connected), red (disconnected). The app polls the server every 30 seconds
          li The
            strong  Server Manager
            |  shows live resource usage: CPU cores, memory, disk, and running container count per server
          li Right-click a deployed node →
            strong  SSH Terminal
            |  to open a console session to the container on the remote server
          li
            strong  Docker Image Manager
            |  (Simulate → Docker Image Manager) includes a server selector — pull, delete, and manage images on whichever server is active

        h4 Step 8 — Detect & Reconnect
        p If you close and reopen the app:
        ul
          li Go to
            strong  Simulate → Detect Running Labs
            |  — it scans the active remote server for running containerlab topologies
          li Select a lab to reconnect and resume your session
          li The app also
            strong  auto-reconnects
            |  to the last active lab and server on startup (stored in browser localStorage)

        h4 Step 9 — Teardown
        p Go to
          strong  Simulate → Destroy Containerlab
          |  to stop and remove all containers for the current topology on the remote server.

        h4 How It Works Internally
        ul
          li Docker commands run transparently on the remote host via
            code  DOCKER_HOST=ssh://user@host
          li For password auth, an SSH_ASKPASS helper supplies credentials to the Docker SSH transport automatically
          li Topology files are uploaded via SFTP (ssh2) to the remote lab directory
          li Deploy/destroy execute via
            code  ssh -t user@host 'sudo clab deploy ...'
          li Container console opens via
            code  ssh -t user@host 'docker exec -it ...'
        p
          strong Switching servers:
          |  Use the toolbar indicator or Server Manager to switch the active server at any time. Switching to "Local" routes all operations back to your local machine.

        h3 Physical Port Connections (Host Interfaces)
        p Connect physical network devices to your virtual containerlab topology by mapping server NICs directly into containers. This is useful for testing virtual switches (e.g. SONiC) with real hardware.

        h4 How It Works
        p Containerlab supports
          code  host:INTERFACE_NAME
          |  link endpoints that bind a physical NIC on the server directly into a container's network namespace. The app provides a dedicated
          strong  Host Port
          |  node type for this.

        h4 Step 1 — Place a Host Port
        p In the device palette (left sidebar), click
          strong  Host Port
          |  (teal node). Click on the canvas to place it. It appears as "Host-Port-1".

        h4 Step 2 — Select Compute Host
        ol
          li Click the Host Port node to open the Properties Panel
          li In the
            strong  Info
            |  tab, select the
            strong  Compute Host
            |  — this determines which server's physical NICs are available
          li Changing the compute host clears all existing ports and links (old server's interfaces are invalid on the new server) and auto-populates with the new server's interfaces
        h4 Step 3 — Configure Ports
        ol
          li Switch to the
            strong  Ports
            |  tab — interfaces from the selected compute host are auto-populated
          li Port status dots reflect the actual interface state:
            strong  green ●
            |  = up,
            strong  grey ○
            |  = down
          li Draw links from the Host Port to your virtual devices — a port picker shows available interfaces from the selected compute host
          li Interfaces already used by other links are disabled in the picker to prevent duplicates
        p
          strong Note:
          |  Virtual and management interfaces (lo, docker0, veth*, br-*, clab*) are automatically filtered out. Only real physical NICs are shown. On macOS, interfaces like utun, awdl, llw, and anpi are also excluded.
        p
          strong Important:
          |  Host interface binding (
          code host:INTERFACE
          | ) only works on
          strong  native Linux
          |  servers. On macOS with Docker Desktop, the containers run inside a Linux VM that cannot access Mac's physical NICs.

        h4 Step 4 — Connect to a Virtual Device
        p Draw a link from the Host Port node to your virtual device (e.g. SONiC switch). The connection works just like any other link on the canvas.

        h4 Step 5 — Deploy
        p Open
          strong  Simulate → Deploy Containerlab
          | . The YAML preview shows the correct format:
        .help-example
          .help-example-title Generated YAML with host and bridge endpoints
          | topology:
          |   kinds:
          |     sonic-vs:
          |       image: netreplica/docker-sonic-vs:latest
          |   nodes:
          |     sonic-1:
          |       kind: sonic-vs
          |   links:
          |     - endpoints: ["sonic-1:eth1", "host:enp3s0"]
          |     - endpoints: ["sonic-1:eth2", "host:enp4s0"]
          |     - endpoints: ["sonic-1:eth3", "bridge:br-fabric"]
        p Host Port and Bridge nodes do
          strong  NOT
          |  appear in the nodes: section — they only affect link endpoints.

        h4 Validation Warnings
        ul
          li
            strong No interface selected
            |  — "1 host port(s) without interface assigned: Host-Port-1"
          li
            strong Duplicate interface
            |  — "Duplicate host interface enp3s0 used by: Host-Port-1, Host-Port-2"

        h4 Requirements
        ul
          li The physical NIC must be
            strong  unused
            |  (not the server's management interface)
          li The physical device must be
            strong  cabled
            |  to that NIC on the server
          li The server needs multiple NICs — management + lab-facing ports

        h3 Bridge Connections
        p Connect virtual containerlab nodes to Linux bridges, libvirt virtual networks, or OVS bridges on the server. This is useful for connecting containers to VMs or external networks.

        h4 How It Works
        p Containerlab supports
          code  bridge:BRIDGE_NAME
          |  link endpoints that attach a container interface to an existing bridge on the host. The app provides a dedicated
          strong  Bridge
          |  node type (purple) for this.

        h4 Step 1 — Place a Bridge Node
        p In the device palette (left sidebar), click
          strong  Bridge
          |  (purple node). Click on the canvas to place it. It appears as "Bridge-1".

        h4 Step 2 — Select Compute Host & Bridge
        ol
          li Click the Bridge node to open the Properties Panel
          li In the
            strong  Info
            |  tab, select the
            strong  Compute Host
            |  — this determines which server's bridges are discovered
          li The
            strong  Bridge
            |  dropdown auto-fetches available bridges from the selected compute host (Linux bridges, libvirt networks, and OVS bridges)
          li Select the bridge you want to connect to (e.g.
            code  virbr0
            | ,
            code  br-fabric
            | )
          li Click ↻ to refresh the bridge list
          li Changing the compute host re-fetches bridges from the new server

        h4 Step 3 — Connect to a Virtual Device
        p Draw a link from the Bridge node to your virtual device (e.g. SONiC switch). Each Bridge node has 4 ports (br0–br3) for multiple connections.

        h4 Step 4 — Deploy
        p The generated YAML uses
          code  bridge:BRIDGE_NAME
          |  as the link endpoint. Bridge nodes do not appear in the
          code  nodes:
          |  section.

        h4 Validation Warnings
        ul
          li
            strong No bridge selected
            |  — warns if a Bridge node has no bridge name assigned
          li
            strong Bridge does not exist
            |  — pre-deploy validation checks that the bridge actually exists on the target server

        h3 Bridge Manager
        p
          strong Simulate → Bridge Manager…
          |  opens a dialog to discover and manage Linux bridges, libvirt virtual networks, and OVS bridges on the active server.

        h4 Features
        ul
          li
            strong Bridge list:
            |  Shows all bridges with name, type badge (libvirt/linux/ovs), state, subnet, and member interfaces
          li
            strong Create bridge:
            |  Click "+ Create Bridge" to open the creation form. Select bridge type:
            ul
              li
                span.help-tag Libvirt
                |  — creates a libvirt virtual network with optional NAT/isolated/bridge mode, DHCP subnet, and autostart
              li
                span.help-tag Linux
                |  — creates a plain Linux bridge via
                span.help-code  ip link
              li
                span.help-tag OVS
                |  — creates an Open vSwitch bridge via
                span.help-code  ovs-vsctl
          li
            strong Start / Stop:
            |  Start or stop libvirt networks
          li
            strong Delete:
            |  Remove bridges (libvirt, Linux, or OVS)
          li
            strong Refresh:
            |  Reload bridge list from the server

        h3 VXLAN Tunnel Setup
        p
          strong Simulate → VXLAN Tunnel…
          |  opens a dialog for setting up VXLAN tunnels between servers. This is useful for multi-server topologies where cross-server links need Layer 2 connectivity.
        h4 Fields
        table.help-table
          thead: tr
            th Field
            th Description
          tbody
            tr
              td Bridge Name
              td The bridge to attach the VXLAN interface to
            tr
              td Remote IP
              td IP address of the remote server endpoint
            tr
              td VNI
              td VXLAN Network Identifier (unique per tunnel)
            tr
              td Method
              td Choose between
                span.help-code  ip link
                |  (standard Linux) or
                span.help-code  ovs-vsctl
                |  (OVS)

        h3 Pre-deploy Validation
        p When you open the Deploy Containerlab dialog, the app automatically runs pre-deploy validation to verify that host interfaces and bridges referenced in the topology actually exist on the target servers.
        h4 What It Checks
        ul
          li
            strong Host interfaces:
            |  Verifies each assigned physical NIC exists on the target server. Reports interface state (up/down), IP assignment warnings, and bridge membership conflicts.
          li
            strong Bridges:
            |  Verifies each assigned bridge exists on the target server.
        h4 Validation Results
        ul
          li
            span(style='color:#ef4444') ✖ Errors
            |  (red) — block deployment. Fix the issue before deploying.
          li
            span(style='color:#f59e0b') ⚠ Warnings
            |  (yellow) — informational but allow deployment.
          li
            span(style='color:#22c55e') ✔ All OK
            |  (green) — all interfaces and bridges validated.
        p Click
          strong  Re-validate
          |  to re-run the checks after making changes. The Deploy button is disabled while validation errors exist.

        h3 Platform Setup (macOS vs Linux)
        table.help-table
          thead: tr
            th Component
            th macOS (Apple Silicon / Intel)
            th Linux (x86_64)
          tbody
            tr
              td Docker
              td Docker Desktop (
                span.help-code brew install --cask docker
                | )
              td Docker Engine (
                span.help-code curl -fsSL https://get.docker.com | sh
                | )
            tr
              td Containerlab
              td Docker wrapper — run via container (see below)
              td Native install:
                span.help-code bash -c "$(curl -sL https://get.containerlab.dev)"
            tr
              td crpd
              td ✅ Supported
              td ✅ Supported
            tr
              td cEOS / SRL
              td ✅ Supported
              td ✅ Supported
            tr
              td SONiC-VS
              td ✅ Supported
              td ✅ Supported
            tr
              td vrnetlab VMs (vQFX, vJunos)
              td ❌ Requires KVM — not available on Mac
              td ✅ Requires nested virtualization (KVM)
        h4 Image Architecture
        p
          strong Important:
          |  Docker images must match your host CPU architecture. Apple Silicon Macs (M1/M2/M3/M4) use
          strong  arm64
          | , while Intel Macs and most Linux servers use
          strong  amd64
          | . Loading an amd64 image on Apple Silicon causes Docker Desktop to crash.
        p
          | The Deploy dialog will detect and warn you if an image has the wrong architecture. Download the correct variant:
        ul
          li
            strong Apple Silicon Mac:
            |  Download the
            span.help-code  arm64
            |  or
            span.help-code  aarch64
            |  variant (e.g.
            span.help-code  junos-routing-crpd-arm64-25.4R1.12.tgz
            | )
          li
            strong Intel Mac / Linux:
            |  Download the
            span.help-code  amd64
            |  or
            span.help-code  x86_64
            |  variant
        p
          strong macOS containerlab setup:
          |  Since containerlab does not install natively on macOS, run it via the official Docker wrapper. Add this alias to your
          |
          span.help-code ~/.zshrc
          | :
        .help-example
          .help-example-title containerlab Docker wrapper alias
          span.help-code alias containerlab='docker run --rm -it --privileged --network host -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock -v /var/run/netns:/var/run/netns -v $(pwd):/lab --pid host ghcr.io/srl-labs/clab containerlab'
          br
          span.help-code alias clab='containerlab'

        h3 cRPD Lab (Recommended for macOS)
        p
          |  cRPD (Containerized Routing Protocol Daemon) is the recommended Juniper kind for macOS. It runs as a lightweight Docker container — no KVM/nested virtualization needed. Boots in ~30 seconds with full Junos CLI.
        p
          strong cRPD capabilities:
        ul
          li Full Junos CLI (
            span.help-code show route
            | ,
            |
            span.help-code show bgp summary
            | ,
            |
            span.help-code show configuration
            | )
          li BGP, OSPF, IS-IS, MPLS LDP, SR-MPLS, EVPN-VXLAN control plane
          li Route policies, prefix lists, community filters
          li SNMP and Syslog support
          li Not supported: data plane forwarding (control plane only)
        p
          strong Step 1 — Get the cRPD image:
        p
          strong Important:
          |  Download the correct architecture for your platform —
          strong  arm64
          |  for Apple Silicon Mac,
          strong  amd64
          |  for Intel Mac / Linux. Loading the wrong architecture will crash Docker Desktop.
        p
          |  Option A — Import from the app (recommended):
        ul
          li Download the cRPD
            span.help-code  .tgz
            |  file from
            |
            a(href='https://www.juniper.net/us/en/products/routers/containerized-routing-protocol-daemon-crpd.html' target='_blank') juniper.net/crpd
          li Open
            strong  Simulate → Deploy Containerlab
          li In the Docker Images section, click
            strong  Import Image File
          li Select your
            span.help-code  .tgz
            |  file — the app automatically:
          ul
            li Fixes Juniper's broken symlink issue in the archive
            li Loads the image via
              span.help-code  docker load
            li Auto-tags it as
              span.help-code  crpd:latest
          li The image appears with ✓ in the Docker Images list
        p
          |  Option B — Pull from Juniper container registry:
        .help-example
          .help-example-title Pull from registry
          span.help-code docker login enterprise-hub.juniper.net
          br
          span.help-code docker pull enterprise-hub.juniper.net/crpd:latest
          br
          span.help-code docker tag enterprise-hub.juniper.net/crpd:latest crpd:latest
        p
          |  Option C — Manual terminal load (if the app import fails):
        .help-example
          .help-example-title Fix symlink and load manually
          span.help-code mkdir -p /tmp/crpd-fix && cd /tmp/crpd-fix
          br
          span.help-code tar xzf ~/Downloads/junos-routing-crpd-arm64-25.4R1.12.tgz
          br
          span.help-code LAYER_DIR=$(ls -d */ | head -1) && LAYER_TAR=$(ls *.tar | head -1)
          br
          span.help-code rm "$LAYER_DIR/layer.tar" && cp "$LAYER_TAR" "$LAYER_DIR/layer.tar"
          br
          span.help-code tar cf /tmp/crpd-fixed.tar . && docker load -i /tmp/crpd-fixed.tar
          br
          span.help-code docker tag crpd:25.4R1.12 crpd:latest
        p
          strong Step 2 — Set up topology in the app:
        ul
          li Add Router or Switch nodes to the canvas
          li Set
            |
            span.help-code Vendor = Juniper
          li Leave
            |
            span.help-code Device Type
            |  and
            |
            span.help-code Model
            |  empty — this auto-maps to
            |
            span.help-code crpd
          li The containerlab kind hint shows:
            |
            span.help-code Containerlab: crpd · Image: crpd:latest · Boot: ~30 sec
          li Create links between nodes and optionally auto-assign IPs
        p
          strong Step 3 — Deploy:
          |  Simulate → Deploy Containerlab. The app generates
          |
          span.help-code .clab.yml
          |  with
          |
          span.help-code kind: crpd
          |  for all Juniper nodes. Nodes boot in ~30 seconds.
        p
          strong Step 4 — Interact:
        ul
          li
            span.help-code Inspect Lab Status
            |  — verify containers are running (green status dots)
          li
            span.help-code Right-click → Open Console
            |  — opens Junos CLI (
            span.help-code cli
            | )
          li
            span.help-code Fetch Config
            |  — retrieves running config via
            |
            span.help-code show configuration | display set
          li
            span.help-code Push Config
            |  — pushes Junos set commands into the running container
          li
            span.help-code BGP Polling
            |  — live status via
            |
            span.help-code show bgp summary
          li
            span.help-code Syslog
            |  — configure with
            |
            span.help-code set system syslog host &lt;ip&gt; port 1514 any info

        h3 Juniper VM Support (vrnetlab) — Linux Only
        p
          |  On Linux hosts with KVM, Juniper nodes are automatically mapped to vrnetlab VM kinds based on the Device Type selected in node properties:
        table.help-table
          thead: tr
            th Device Type
            th Model Examples
            th Containerlab Kind
            th Default Image
            th Boot Time
          tbody
            tr
              td QFX
              td QFX5120-48Y, QFX5230-64CD
              td juniper_vqfx
              td vrnetlab/vr-vqfx:latest
              td ~7 min
            tr
              td EX
              td EX4400-48T, EX9200
              td juniper_vjunosswitch
              td vrnetlab/vr-vjunosswitch:latest
              td ~15 min
            tr
              td MX
              td MX204, MX960
              td juniper_vjunosrouter
              td vrnetlab/vr-vjunosrouter:latest
              td ~15 min
            tr
              td PTX
              td PTX10001, PTX10008
              td juniper_vjunosrouter
              td vrnetlab/vr-vjunosrouter:latest
              td ~15 min
            tr
              td ACX
              td ACX7100, ACX7509
              td juniper_vjunosrouter
              td vrnetlab/vr-vjunosrouter:latest
              td ~15 min
            tr
              td (none selected)
              td (model blank)
              td crpd
              td crpd:latest
              td ~30 sec
        p
          strong Containerlab kind hint:
          |  When you select a Juniper Device Type, a hint box appears below the model field showing the mapped containerlab kind, Docker image, and expected boot time. If you set a custom Image / Template field, the hint updates to reflect your override.
        p
          strong Per-node image override:
          |  Set the Image / Template field in node properties to use a specific version (e.g., vrnetlab/vr-vjunosswitch:23.2R1.15) instead of the default.
        p
          strong vrnetlab image building:
          |  vrnetlab images are not on Docker Hub. Build them locally from vendor qcow2 VM images:
        .help-example
          .help-example-title Build vrnetlab image (example: vQFX)
          span.help-code git clone https://github.com/srl-labs/vrnetlab.git
          br
          span.help-code cd vrnetlab/juniper/vqfx
          br
          span.help-code # Copy vqfx qcow2 files into this directory
          br
          span.help-code make
        p
          |  See
          |
          a(href='https://containerlab.dev/manual/vrnetlab/' target='_blank') containerlab vrnetlab docs
          |  for full instructions.
        p
          strong Requirements:
          |  Linux x86_64 with nested virtualization (KVM) enabled. Allocate at least 4 GB RAM per vrnetlab VM node. Not available on macOS (no KVM support). Use crpd on Mac for Juniper labs.

        h3 Cisco IOS-XR Lab (XRd — Recommended)
        p
          |  XRd (IOS-XR Container) is the recommended Cisco kind for containerlab. It runs as a lightweight Docker container with full IOS-XR CLI. Boots in ~2 minutes.
        p
          strong XRd capabilities:
        ul
          li Full IOS-XR CLI (
            span.help-code show ip route
            | ,
            |
            span.help-code show bgp summary
            | ,
            |
            span.help-code show running-config
            | )
          li BGP, OSPF, IS-IS, MPLS LDP, SR-MPLS, Segment Routing, EVPN
          li Route policies, prefix sets, community sets
          li SNMP, Syslog, gRPC/gNMI telemetry
          li Not supported: full data plane forwarding (control plane only)
        p
          strong Step 1 — Get the XRd image:
        p
          |  Option A — Pull from Cisco container registry:
        .help-example
          .help-example-title Pull XRd from registry
          span.help-code docker login &lt;cisco-registry&gt;
          br
          span.help-code docker pull ios-xr/xrd-control-plane:latest
        p
          |  Option B — Use the Docker Image Manager:
        ul
          li Open
            strong  Simulate → Docker Image Manager
          li Type
            |
            span.help-code  ios-xr/xrd-control-plane:latest
            |  in the pull input and click
            strong  Pull Image
          li Or click
            strong  Import Image File
            |  to load from a
            |
            span.help-code .tar
            |  /
            |
            span.help-code .tgz
            |  file
        p
          |  Option C — Download from Cisco Software Center:
        ul
          li Visit
            |
            a(href='https://software.cisco.com' target='_blank')  software.cisco.com
            |  (requires valid Cisco CCO account)
          li Download the XRd Control Plane container image
          li Load via terminal:
        .help-example
          span.help-code docker load -i xrd-control-plane-container-x64.tgz
          br
          span.help-code docker tag ios-xr/xrd-control-plane:7.11.1 ios-xr/xrd-control-plane:latest
        p
          strong Step 2 — Set up topology in the app:
        ul
          li Drag Router nodes onto the canvas
          li Set
            |
            span.help-code  Vendor = Cisco
          li Set
            |
            span.help-code  Model = XRD
            |  (or leave blank — Cisco defaults to XRd)
          li The containerlab kind hint shows:
            |
            span.help-code  Containerlab: cisco_xrd · Image: ios-xr/xrd-control-plane:latest · Boot: ~2 min
          li Create links between nodes and optionally auto-assign IPs
        p
          strong Step 3 — Deploy:
          |  Simulate → Deploy Containerlab. The app generates
          |
          span.help-code .clab.yml
          |  with
          |
          span.help-code kind: cisco_xrd
          |  for all Cisco XRd nodes. Nodes boot in ~2 minutes.
        p
          strong Step 4 — Interact:
        ul
          li
            span.help-code Inspect Lab Status
            |  — verify containers are running (green status dots)
          li
            span.help-code Right-click → Open Console
            |  — opens IOS-XR CLI
          li
            span.help-code Fetch Config
            |  — retrieves running config via
            |
            span.help-code  show running-config
          li
            span.help-code Push Config
            |  — pushes IOS-XR configuration commands into the running container

        h3 Cisco VM Support (vrnetlab) — Linux Only
        p
          |  On Linux hosts with KVM, Cisco nodes can run as full VM-based containers via vrnetlab. These require qcow2 images built into Docker containers.
        table.help-table
          thead: tr
            th Model Prefix
            th Containerlab Kind
            th Default Image
            th Boot Time
            th Platform
          tbody
            tr
              td XRD / XR / IOS-XR
              td cisco_xrd
              td ios-xr/xrd-control-plane:latest
              td ~2 min
              td IOS-XR (container)
            tr
              td XRV
              td cisco_xrv9k
              td vrnetlab/vr-xrv9k:latest
              td ~10 min
              td IOS-XR (VM)
            tr
              td CSR / IOS-XE / C8000
              td cisco_csr1000v
              td vrnetlab/vr-csr:latest
              td ~7 min
              td IOS-XE (VM)
            tr
              td N9K / NEXUS
              td cisco_n9kv
              td vrnetlab/vr-n9kv:latest
              td ~10 min
              td NX-OS (VM)
        p
          strong vrnetlab image building:
          |  vrnetlab images are not on Docker Hub. Build them locally from Cisco qcow2 VM images:
        .help-example
          .help-example-title Build vrnetlab image (example: XRv9k)
          span.help-code git clone https://github.com/srl-labs/vrnetlab.git
          br
          span.help-code cd vrnetlab/cisco/xrv9k
          br
          span.help-code # Copy xrv9k qcow2 file into this directory
          br
          span.help-code make
        p
          |  See
          |
          a(href='https://containerlab.dev/manual/vrnetlab/' target='_blank') containerlab vrnetlab docs
          |  for full instructions.
        p
          strong Requirements:
          |  Linux x86_64 with nested virtualization (KVM) enabled. Allocate at least 8 GB RAM for XRv9k, 4 GB for CSR/N9Kv. Not available on macOS (no KVM support). Use XRd container on Mac for Cisco IOS-XR labs.

        h3 SONiC-VS Lab
        p
          |  SONiC (Software for Open Networking in the Cloud) is an open-source network OS. The
          strong  SONiC-VS
          |  (Virtual Switch) image runs as a lightweight container — ideal for simulating data-centre leaf/spine fabrics with BGP, ECMP, and VXLAN.
        table.help-table
          thead: tr
            th Property
            th Value
          tbody
            tr
              td Containerlab kind
              td
                code sonic-vs
            tr
              td Default image
              td
                code netreplica/docker-sonic-vs:latest
            tr
              td Boot time
              td ~3 minutes
            tr
              td Platform
              td macOS (ARM/Intel) and Linux (x86_64)
            tr
              td Default credentials
              td admin / YourPaSsWoRd
        p
          strong Capabilities:
        ul
          li BGP (eBGP/iBGP), OSPF, static routes
          li ECMP, VXLAN, EVPN
          li Port channels, VLANs, ACLs
          li SNMP, Syslog, gNMI telemetry
          li config_db.json configuration model
          li FRR-based routing stack

        p
          strong Step 1 — Get the SONiC-VS image:
        p
          |  Option A — Pull from Docker Hub (recommended):
        .help-example
          .help-example-title Pull SONiC-VS
          span.help-code docker pull netreplica/docker-sonic-vs:latest
        p
          |  Option B — Use the Docker Image Manager:
        ul
          li Open
            strong  Simulate → Docker Image Manager
          li Type
            |
            span.help-code  netreplica/docker-sonic-vs:latest
            |  in the pull input and click
            strong  Pull Image
          li Real-time progress is displayed as the image downloads
          li Or click
            strong  Import Image File
            |  to load from a
            |
            span.help-code .tar
            | /
            span.help-code .tgz
            |  file
        p
          |  Option C — Pull during Deploy:
        ul
          li Open
            strong  Simulate → Deploy Containerlab
          li The app auto-detects SONiC nodes and checks for the required image
          li If the image is missing, click
            strong  Pull
            |  next to the image name in the image checklist
          li Download progress is shown in real-time

        p
          strong Step 2 — Set up topology in the app:
        ul
          li Drag Router or Switch nodes onto the canvas
          li Set
            |
            span.help-code  Vendor = SONiC
            |  (or
            span.help-code  Edgecore
            | )
          li The containerlab kind hint shows:
            |
            span.help-code  Containerlab: sonic-vs · Image: netreplica/docker-sonic-vs:latest · Boot: ~3 min
          li Create links between nodes and assign IPs (Auto-Assign IP recommended)
          li Set
            strong  ASN
            |  on each node for BGP — nodes without ASN will not have BGP configured

        p
          strong Step 3 — Deploy:
        ul
          li Open
            strong  Simulate → Deploy Containerlab
          li The app generates config_db.json for each SONiC node with:
        ul
          li Port configuration (Ethernet0, Ethernet4, Ethernet8… in 4-lane steps)
          li Interface IP addresses from the canvas
          li BGP configuration with ASN and neighbor peering
          li Loopback interface with router-id
        ul
          li Click
            strong  Deploy
            |  — topology YAML and configs are uploaded and containers are started
          li Wait ~3 minutes for SONiC services to fully boot

        p
          strong Step 4 — Post-deploy:
        ul
          li The app automatically enables interfaces on SONiC containers after boot (~2 min timer)
          li Use
            strong  Inspect Lab Status
            |  to see management IPs
          li Right-click a node →
            strong  Open SSH Terminal
            |  (credentials: admin / YourPaSsWoRd)
          li Verify BGP:
        .help-example
          .help-example-title SONiC CLI commands
          span.help-code show ip bgp summary
          br
          span.help-code show ip route
          br
          span.help-code show interfaces status
          br
          span.help-code show runningconfiguration all

        h3 Docker Image Manager
        p
          |  The Docker Image Manager (
          strong Simulate → Docker Image Manager
          | ) provides a central interface to manage container images on local or remote servers.
        p
          strong Features:
        ul
          li
            strong Server selector:
            |  Switch between Local and any configured remote server to manage images on that host
          li
            strong Image list:
            |  Shows all Docker images with name, tag, size, architecture, and age
          li
            strong Pull:
            |  Type any image name (e.g.
            span.help-code  netreplica/docker-sonic-vs:latest
            | ) and click Pull Image — real-time download progress is streamed from Docker
          li
            strong Import:
            |  Click Import Image File to load a
            span.help-code  .tar
            | /
            span.help-code .tgz
            | /
            span.help-code .gz
            |  file — progress is shown during loading
          li
            strong Tag:
            |  Retag an existing image (e.g. add a
            span.help-code :latest
            |  alias)
          li
            strong Delete:
            |  Remove images you no longer need. If an image is in use by a container, the app automatically retries with force-delete
          li
            strong Refresh:
            |  Reload the image list from Docker
        p
          strong Pull progress:
          |  When pulling or loading images, Docker progress is streamed in real-time — you can see each layer downloading with size and speed. This replaces the previous static "Loading…" message.

    //- ── KVM & Virtual Machines ───────────
    .help-section
      button.help-section-title(type='button', (click)='toggleHelpSection("kvm")')
        span.help-chevron {{ isSectionOpen('kvm') ? '▾' : '▸' }}
        | KVM & Virtual Machines
      .help-section-body(*ngIf='isSectionOpen("kvm")')
        h3 Overview
        p
          | Many network OS images (Cisco XRv9k, Juniper vQFX/vMX, Nokia SR OS) run as full virtual machines inside Docker containers via
          strong  vrnetlab
          | . These require KVM (hardware virtualization) on the host server. This section covers checking KVM readiness, building vrnetlab images, and managing VMs directly via libvirt.

        h3 KVM Readiness Check
        p
          | The Server Manager automatically detects KVM support on each server. Open
          strong  Simulate → Server Manager
          |  and look at the
          strong  KVM
          |  column:
        ul
          li
            strong ✓ (green):
            |  KVM is available —
            span.help-code /dev/kvm
            |  exists on the server. This server can run vrnetlab VM-backed nodes.
          li
            strong ✗ (red):
            |  KVM is not available. Container-only images work fine (SRL, cEOS, crpd, SONiC, Alpine), but vrnetlab VMs will not boot.
          li
            strong VMs column:
            |  Shows the count of running libvirt VMs on the server, polled every 10 seconds.
        p
          strong How KVM is detected:
        ul
          li
            strong Linux:
            |  Checks for
            span.help-code  /dev/kvm
            |  device file (KVM kernel module loaded)
          li
            strong macOS:
            |  Checks
            span.help-code  sysctl kern.hv_support
            |  (Hypervisor.framework — note: containerlab vrnetlab VMs still require Linux KVM)
          li
            strong Remote servers:
            |  Checks via SSH during the server test and during resource polling
        p
          strong Tip:
          |  If KVM shows ✗ on a Linux server, ensure the KVM kernel module is loaded:
        .help-example
          span.help-code sudo modprobe kvm_intel   # Intel CPUs
          br
          span.help-code sudo modprobe kvm_amd     # AMD CPUs
          br
          span.help-code ls -la /dev/kvm           # Verify device exists

        h3 Building vrnetlab Images
        p
          | vrnetlab converts vendor VM disk images (qcow2, vmdk) into Docker containers that containerlab can deploy. This is required for VM-based NOS platforms.
        h4 Supported Vendors
        table.help-table
          thead: tr
            th Vendor
            th Kind
            th Disk Format
            th Typical Boot Time
          tbody
            tr
              td Cisco XRv9000
              td
                span.help-code cisco_xrv9k
              td qcow2
              td ~10-15 min
            tr
              td Cisco XRd
              td
                span.help-code cisco_xrd
              td qcow2
              td ~5-8 min
            tr
              td Cisco CSR1000v
              td
                span.help-code cisco_csr1000v
              td qcow2
              td ~5-8 min
            tr
              td Cisco Nexus 9000v
              td
                span.help-code cisco_n9kv
              td qcow2
              td ~10-15 min
            tr
              td Juniper vQFX
              td
                span.help-code juniper_vqfx
              td qcow2 / vmdk
              td ~7-10 min
            tr
              td Juniper vJunos Router
              td
                span.help-code juniper_vjunosrouter
              td qcow2
              td ~7-10 min
            tr
              td Juniper vJunos Switch
              td
                span.help-code juniper_vjunosswitch
              td qcow2
              td ~7-10 min
            tr
              td Nokia SR OS
              td
                span.help-code nokia_sros
              td qcow2
              td ~5-8 min
            tr
              td Arista vEOS
              td
                span.help-code arista_veos
              td vmdk / iso
              td ~5-8 min

        h4 Build Workflow
        p
          strong Step 1 — Open the Builder:
        ul
          li Go to
            strong  Simulate → Docker Image Manager
          li Click the
            strong  Build vrnetlab Image
            |  button (next to Import Image File)
        p
          strong Step 2 — Select Vendor:
        ul
          li Choose the vendor/platform from the dropdown (e.g. "Cisco XRv9000")
          li The builder knows which vrnetlab subdirectory and Makefile to use
        p
          strong Step 3 — Select VM Image File:
        ul
          li Click
            strong  Browse…
            |  and select your vendor VM disk file
          li Common file types:
            span.help-code  .qcow2
            | ,
            span.help-code  .vmdk
            | ,
            span.help-code  .iso
          li
            strong Important:
            |  vrnetlab's launch.py only searches for
            span.help-code  .qcow2
            |  files at boot. If you have an ISO image, the Docker build may succeed but the VM will not start. Always use the qcow2 format when available
        p
          strong Step 4 — Build:
        ul
          li Click
            strong  Build Image
          li A terminal window opens showing real-time build progress:
        .help-example
          span.help-code === vrnetlab Image Builder ===
          br
          span.help-code Vendor: Cisco XRv9000
          br
          span.help-code Image: xrv9k-fullk9-x-7.9.1.qcow2
          br
          span.help-code
          br
          span.help-code Cloning vrnetlab repository...
          br
          span.help-code Copying VM image to xrv9k/...
          br
          span.help-code Building Docker image (this may take several minutes)...
        ul
          li First build clones the vrnetlab repository to
            span.help-code  ~/.tlink-netops/vrnetlab/
            |  (subsequent builds reuse it)
          li The build copies your VM image into the vendor subdirectory and runs
            span.help-code  make docker-image
          li Build time varies by vendor — typically 5-20 minutes depending on image size
        p
          strong Step 5 — Verify:
        ul
          li After the build completes, close the terminal
          li The new Docker image appears in the Docker Image Manager (e.g.
            span.help-code  vrnetlab/vr-xrv9k:7.9.1
            | )
          li When you add a matching node type on the canvas, it will use this image automatically

        h3 VM Manager (libvirt/virsh)
        p
          | For servers where you want to run VMs directly via libvirt/QEMU — outside of containerlab — use the VM Manager. This provides a GUI over
          span.help-code  virsh
          |  commands.
        h4 Opening the VM Manager
        p Go to
          strong  Simulate → VM Manager (KVM)…
          |  from the menu. The dialog operates on the currently active server (local or remote via SSH).

        h4 Viewing VMs
        p
          | The VM list shows all VMs on the active server with their current state:
        ul
          li
            span(style='color:#22c55e') ●
            strong  running
            |  — VM is active and operational
          li
            span(style='color:#f59e0b') ●
            strong  paused
            |  — VM is suspended in memory
          li
            span(style='color:#6b7280') ●
            strong  shut off
            |  — VM is stopped
        p Click
          strong  Refresh
          |  to reload the VM list from the server.

        h4 VM Actions
        table.help-table
          thead: tr
            th Action
            th Description
            th When Available
          tbody
            tr
              td Start
              td
                span.help-code virsh start
                |  — Boot the VM
              td When shut off
            tr
              td Stop
              td
                span.help-code virsh shutdown
                |  — Graceful ACPI shutdown
              td When running
            tr
              td Console
              td Opens a terminal with
                span.help-code  virsh console
                |  for serial access
              td When running
            tr
              td Snapshots
              td Expand/collapse inline snapshot list for the VM
              td Always
            tr
              td ⚡ Autostart
              td
                span.help-code virsh autostart
                |  — Enable the VM to start automatically on host boot
              td When running
            tr
              td Force Stop
              td
                span.help-code virsh destroy
                |  — Immediate power-off (no graceful shutdown)
              td When running
            tr
              td Delete
              td
                span.help-code virsh undefine
                |  — Permanently remove the VM definition. Optionally delete associated disk storage.
              td When shut off

        h4 Creating a New VM
        p Click
          strong  + Create VM
          |  to open the creation form:
        table.help-table
          thead: tr
            th Field
            th Description
            th Default
          tbody
            tr
              td Name
              td VM name (used in virsh commands)
              td —
            tr
              td vCPUs
              td Number of virtual CPU cores
              td 2
            tr
              td Memory (MB)
              td RAM allocation in megabytes
              td 2048
            tr
              td Disk Image
              td Path to qcow2/vmdk file — pick from the Server Image Library or click Browse… to select a local file
              td —
            tr
              td Network Bridge
              td Linux bridge for VM networking (includes dynamically discovered bridges)
              td virbr0
        p
          strong Disk Image Library:
          |  When creating a VM, the form shows a "Server Images" panel listing all disk images in
          |
          span.help-code /var/lib/libvirt/images
          |  on the active server. Click an image to select it. Features:
        ul
          li
            strong Image list:
            |  Shows file name and size for each image on the server
          li
            strong Upload:
            |  Click ⬆ Upload to pick a local qcow2/vmdk file and upload it to the server's
            span.help-code  /var/lib/libvirt/images
            |  directory. For remote servers, the file is transferred via SFTP then moved with sudo.
          li
            strong Refresh:
            |  Click ↻ to reload the image list
          li
            strong Browse:
            |  Click Browse… to select a file from the local filesystem directly (bypasses the server image library)
        p
          | Click
          strong  Create & Start
          |  to define and boot the VM. The app generates a libvirt XML domain definition, runs
          span.help-code  virsh define
          |  and
          span.help-code  virsh start
          | . For remote servers, the XML is uploaded via SFTP.

        h4 VM Snapshots
        p
          | Click
          strong  Snapshots
          |  on any VM to expand the inline snapshot panel:
        ul
          li View existing snapshots with names and creation dates
          li Click
            strong  + New Snapshot
            |  to create a snapshot of the current VM state (
            span.help-code virsh snapshot-create-as
            | )
          li Click
            strong  Restore
            |  to revert a VM to a previous snapshot (
            span.help-code virsh snapshot-revert
            | )
          li Click
            strong  🗑
            |  (delete) to permanently remove a snapshot (
            span.help-code virsh snapshot-delete
            | )
          li Snapshots capture the full VM state including memory and disk — useful for saving known-good configurations

        h4 Deleting a VM
        p
          | Click
          strong  Delete
          |  on a shut-off VM to open the deletion confirmation dialog. Options:
        ul
          li
            strong Confirm Delete:
            |  Permanently removes the VM definition via
            span.help-code  virsh undefine
          li
            strong Also delete disk image:
            |  Check this option to also remove the associated disk storage (
            span.help-code virsh undefine --remove-all-storage
            | )

        h4 End-to-End Example
        p
          strong Scenario:
          |  You want to run a Cisco XRv9000 router on a remote server with KVM support.
        ol
          li Open
            strong  Server Manager
            |  → verify
            span.help-code  san-ft-ai-srv02
            |  shows KVM ✓
          li Open
            strong  Docker Image Manager
            |  → click
            strong  Build vrnetlab Image
          li Select "Cisco XRv9000" → Browse for your
            span.help-code  xrv9k-7.9.1.qcow2
            |  → click
            strong  Build Image
          li Wait for the build to complete in the terminal window
          li On the canvas, add a Cisco XRv9k router node — it picks up the new Docker image
          li Deploy via
            strong  Simulate → Deploy Containerlab
            |  targeting the KVM-enabled server
          li Alternatively, use
            strong  VM Manager
            |  to create a standalone VM directly with libvirt (no containerlab needed)

        h4 Prerequisites
        p
          strong For vrnetlab image building:
        ul
          li
            span.help-code git
            |  installed on the machine (to clone the vrnetlab repository)
          li Docker running (the build uses
            span.help-code  docker build
            |  internally)
          li Vendor VM disk image file (qcow2/vmdk — obtained from the vendor portal)
        p
          strong For VM Manager (libvirt):
        ul
          li
            span.help-code libvirt
            |  and
            span.help-code  qemu-kvm
            |  installed on the server
          li
            span.help-code virsh
            |  command available in PATH
          li KVM kernel module loaded (
            span.help-code /dev/kvm
            |  exists)
          li For remote servers: SSH access configured in Server Manager
        .help-example
          .help-example-title Install libvirt on Ubuntu/Debian
          span.help-code sudo apt install -y qemu-kvm libvirt-daemon-system virtinst
          br
          span.help-code sudo systemctl enable --now libvirtd
          br
          span.help-code sudo usermod -aG libvirt $USER
          br
          span.help-code # Log out and back in, then verify:
          br
          span.help-code virsh list --all

    //- ── Monitoring & Automation ───────────
    .help-section
      button.help-section-title(type='button', (click)='toggleHelpSection("monitoring")')
        span.help-chevron {{ isSectionOpen('monitoring') ? '▾' : '▸' }}
        | Monitoring & Automation
      .help-section-body(*ngIf='isSectionOpen("monitoring")')
        p Workflow: SSH Setup → Device Mapping → Polling → Alarms → Config Backup → Event Rules → Upgrade Plans.

        p
          strong 1. SSH Setup
        p Node Info tab → Management IP, Username, Password. "Test SSH" verifies connectivity. Vendor auto-detected if blank. Right-click node → Open SSH Terminal for interactive shell.
        p Vendors: Cisco, Juniper, Arista, Nokia, SONiC, Huawei, HPE, Dell, MikroTik, Extreme.

        p
          strong 2. Device Mapping
        p Devices menu → Import Device CSV. Preview dialog matches by hostname or management IP, shows matched/unmatched counts. Mapped nodes show "M" badge on canvas.

        p
          strong 3. Polling
        p Inventory menu → Poll All / Poll Selected. Auto-Poll intervals: 1, 2, 5, 10, 15, or 30 minutes (first poll runs immediately). Runs vendor-specific commands and detects:
        table.help-table
          thead: tr
            th Alarm
            th Condition
            th Severity
          tbody
            tr
              td High CPU
              td &gt;80% (&gt;95% = Critical)
              td Major / Critical
            tr
              td High Memory
              td &gt;85% (&gt;95% = Critical)
              td Major / Critical
            tr
              td Interface Down
              td Mapped port goes down
              td Warning
            tr
              td Vendor Alarms
              td Parsed from CLI output
              td Per device
            tr
              td Config Drift
              td Config hash changed since backup
              td Warning
        p Alarms auto-clear when resolved. Interface states sync to front-panel port diagram.

        p
          strong Inventory Panel:
          |  Toggle via Inventory menu → Inventory Panel. Shows a sidebar table with Device / OS Version / Model / Last Polled columns. Quick-action buttons: Poll All (↻) and Toggle Auto-Poll (⟳). Rows with active alarms are highlighted.
        p
          strong 4. Alarm Panel
        p Inventory menu → Alarm Panel. Active alarm count shown in status bar (click to toggle panel); critical nodes get a red badge on canvas.
        p
          strong Severity filter:
          |  All / Critical / Major / Minor / Warning / Info.
        p
          strong Category filter:
          |  All / Interface Down / High CPU / High Memory / Config Drift / Reachability / Version Mismatch / Custom.
        p
          strong Actions:
          |  Per-alarm Acknowledge or Clear buttons. Bulk Ack All / Clear All for batch operations.

        p
          strong 5. Config Backup & Diff
        p Inventory menu → Backup All / Backup Selected. Up to 20 versions per node. Config Diff Viewer compares any two versions side-by-side. Inventory auto-saves to
          |
          span.help-code .inv.json
          |  sidecar.

        p
          strong 6. Event Rules
        p Inventory menu → Event Rules. Each rule: trigger + action + optional node/vendor filters. Enable/disable or delete rules.
        table.help-table
          thead: tr
            th Trigger
            th Fires When
          tbody
            tr
              td: span.help-tag Command Check
              td SSH command output matches regex
            tr
              td: span.help-tag Alarm Raised
              td New alarm detected
            tr
              td: span.help-tag Alarm Cleared
              td Alarm auto-resolves
            tr
              td: span.help-tag Config Change
              td Config hash changed
            tr
              td: span.help-tag Poll Complete
              td Poll cycle finishes
            tr
              td: span.help-tag Version Change
              td OS version changed
            tr
              td: span.help-tag Link Down / Up
              td Interface transitions
        p
          strong Actions:
          |
          span.help-tag Notify
          |  ·
          span.help-tag Backup Config
          |  ·
          span.help-tag Run Command
          |  ·
          span.help-tag Log
        p
          strong Filters:
          |  Node =
          |
          span.help-code *
          |  (all),
          |
          span.help-code router-*
          |  (prefix),
          |
          span.help-code ?w-core
          |  (wildcard). Vendor =
          |
          span.help-code *
          |  or specific.
        p Event log at bottom of dialog tracks recent triggers.

        p
          strong 7. Command Check
        p SSHs into matching devices after each poll, runs a custom command, tests output against regex. Match → action fires. No match next poll → alarm auto-clears.
        .help-example
          .help-example-title Examples per vendor
          table.help-table
            thead: tr
              th Vendor
              th Check Command
              th Match Pattern
              th Detects
            tbody
              tr
                td Juniper
                td: span.help-code cli -c "show system alarms"
                td: span.help-code Fan.*Degraded
                td Fan failure
              tr
                td Cisco
                td: span.help-code show environment all
                td: span.help-code Failed|Not Present
                td PSU / fan failure
              tr
                td Arista
                td: span.help-code FastCli -p 15 -c "show system environment all"
                td: span.help-code overheating|powerSupply.*Error
                td Thermal / PSU alert
              tr
                td Nokia
                td: span.help-code show system alarms
                td: span.help-code Critical|Major
                td Any critical alarm
              tr
                td Huawei
                td: span.help-code display alarm active
                td: span.help-code Power.*Abnormal
                td Power alarm
              tr
                td SONiC
                td: span.help-code show system-health summary
                td: span.help-code Not OK
                td Health check failure
              tr
                td MikroTik
                td: span.help-code /system health print
                td: span.help-code temperature.*[5-9]\d
                td High temperature
              tr
                td Dell
                td: span.help-code show environment
                td: span.help-code Failed|Absent
                td Hardware failure
              tr
                td HPE
                td: span.help-code show system
                td: span.help-code fault|failure
                td System fault
              tr
                td Extreme
                td: span.help-code show system
                td: span.help-code Error|Failed
                td System error

        p
          strong 8. Upgrade Plans
        p Inventory menu → Upgrade Plans. Create plan with target firmware, track stages (planned → downloading → staged → upgrading → verifying → completed), run pre/post-check validations.

    //- ── SNMP Polling ────────────────────────
    .help-section
      button.help-section-title(type='button', (click)='toggleHelpSection("snmp")')
        span.help-chevron {{ isSectionOpen('snmp') ? '▾' : '▸' }}
        | SNMP Polling
      .help-section-body(*ngIf='isSectionOpen("snmp")')
        p
          strong Overview:
          |  SNMP polling provides an alternative to SSH for monitoring device metrics. Useful for devices that support SNMP but have limited SSH access, or for lightweight read-only polling.
        p
          strong Setup
          |  — select a node, open the
          |
          span.help-tag Info
          |  tab, and scroll to the SNMP section:
        ul
          li
            span.help-tag Poll Method
            |  — choose between
            |
            span.help-code ssh
            |  (default) and
            |
            span.help-code snmp
          li
            span.help-tag SNMP Version
            |  —
            |
            span.help-code v2c
            |  (community string) or
            |
            span.help-code v3
            |  (auth + privacy)
          li
            span.help-tag Community
            |  (v2c only) — default
            |
            span.help-code public
          li
            span.help-tag Port
            |  — default
            |
            span.help-code 161
          li
            span.help-tag v3 Authentication
            |  — protocol (
            span.help-code MD5
            |  /
            |
            span.help-code SHA
            | ), username, password
          li
            span.help-tag v3 Privacy
            |  — protocol (
            span.help-code DES
            |  /
            |
            span.help-code AES
            | ), password
        p
          strong Test Connection:
          |  Click "Test SNMP" to verify reachability. Sends a
          |
          span.help-code sysDescr.0
          |  (1.3.6.1.2.1.1.1.0) GET request.
        p
          strong Vendor OID Map:
          |  Built-in OID sets for 10 vendors — includes sysDescr, sysName, sysUpTime, ifTable, cpuLoad, and memoryUsed per vendor (Cisco, Juniper, Arista, Nokia, Huawei, SONiC, HPE, Dell, MikroTik, Extreme).
        h3 Vendor SNMP Configuration Examples
        table.help-table
          thead: tr
            th Vendor
            th Enable SNMP
          tbody
            tr
              td Cisco IOS
              td: span.help-code snmp-server community public RO
            tr
              td Cisco NX-OS
              td: span.help-code snmp-server community public use-vrf management
            tr
              td Arista EOS
              td: span.help-code snmp-server community public ro
            tr
              td Juniper Junos
              td: span.help-code set snmp community public authorization read-only
            tr
              td Palo Alto
              td: span.help-code set deviceconfig system snmp-setting access-setting version v2c snmp-community-string public
            tr
              td MikroTik
              td: span.help-code /snmp set enabled=yes
            tr
              td Nokia SR OS
              td: span.help-code configure system snmp community "public" access-permissions r
            tr
              td SONiC
              td: span.help-code sudo config snmp community add public RO
            tr
              td Huawei
              td: span.help-code snmp-agent community read public
            tr
              td Linux (net-snmp)
              td: span.help-code rocommunity public (in /etc/snmp/snmpd.conf)

    //- ── Syslog / Event Streaming ───────────
    .help-section
      button.help-section-title(type='button', (click)='toggleHelpSection("syslog")')
        span.help-chevron {{ isSectionOpen('syslog') ? '▾' : '▸' }}
        | Syslog / Event Streaming
      .help-section-body(*ngIf='isSectionOpen("syslog")')
        p
          strong Overview:
          |  Tlink NetOps includes a built-in UDP syslog receiver (RFC 3164). Network devices forward syslog messages in real time, and they appear in the Syslog Feed panel on the canvas.
        p
          strong Opening the panel:
          |  View menu →
          |
          span.help-tag Syslog Feed
          | . The panel appears at the bottom of the canvas.
        p
          strong Panel controls:
        ul
          li
            span.help-tag ▶ Start
            |  — starts the UDP syslog server on port 1514
          li
            span.help-tag ⏹ Stop
            |  — stops the syslog server
          li
            span.help-tag Status dot
            |  — green pulsing = listening, grey = stopped
          li
            span.help-tag Severity filter
            |  — dropdown to filter by Emergency, Alert, Critical, Error, Warning, Notice, Info, Debug, or All
          li
            span.help-tag ✕ Clear
            |  — clears all received messages
          li
            span.help-tag ? Help
            |  — toggles the vendor configuration hints (always accessible, even when messages are present)
        p
          strong Severity color coding:
        ul
          li
            span.help-tag CRIT
            |  (emergency / alert / critical) — red background
          li
            span.help-tag ERRO
            |  (error) — red text
          li
            span.help-tag WARN
            |  (warning) — yellow text
          li
            span.help-tag INFO
            |  (notice / informational) — blue text
          li
            span.help-tag DEBU
            |  (debug) — muted, reduced opacity
        p
          strong Message fields:
          |  Timestamp (HH:MM:SS) · Hostname · Severity · Message body. Messages cap at 500 entries (oldest discarded).
        p
          strong Port 1514:
          |  The server listens on UDP port 1514 (non-privileged, no sudo needed). If your devices require port 514, set up a port redirect:
        .help-example
          .help-example-title Linux iptables redirect 514 → 1514
          span.help-code sudo iptables -t nat -A PREROUTING -p udp --dport 514 -j REDIRECT --to-port 1514
        p
          strong Auto-detected IP:
          |  The vendor hints panel auto-detects your machine's IP address and pre-fills it in the example commands.
        h3 Vendor Syslog Configuration
        table.help-table
          thead: tr
            th Vendor
            th Commands
          tbody
            tr
              td Cisco IOS / IOS-XE
              td
                span.help-code logging host &lt;ip&gt;
                br
                span.help-code logging trap informational
                br
                span.help-code logging transport udp port 1514
            tr
              td Cisco NX-OS
              td
                span.help-code logging server &lt;ip&gt; 6 port 1514
            tr
              td Arista EOS
              td
                span.help-code logging host &lt;ip&gt; 1514 protocol udp
                br
                span.help-code logging trap informational
            tr
              td Juniper Junos
              td
                span.help-code set system syslog host &lt;ip&gt; port 1514
                br
                span.help-code set system syslog host &lt;ip&gt; any info
            tr
              td Palo Alto PAN-OS
              td
                span.help-code set shared syslog-server-profile tlink server &lt;ip&gt; transport UDP port 1514
            tr
              td MikroTik RouterOS
              td
                span.help-code /system logging action set remote remote=&lt;ip&gt;:1514
                br
                span.help-code /system logging add topics=info action=remote
            tr
              td Linux / rsyslog
              td
                span.help-code *.info @&lt;ip&gt;:1514
                |  (in /etc/rsyslog.d/50-tlink.conf)

    //- ── gRPC/gNMI Telemetry ─────────────
    .help-section
      button.help-section-title(type='button', (click)='toggleHelpSection("telemetry")')
        span.help-chevron {{ isSectionOpen('telemetry') ? '▾' : '▸' }}
        | gRPC/gNMI Telemetry
      .help-section-body(*ngIf='isSectionOpen("telemetry")')
        p
          strong Overview:
          |  gRPC/gNMI telemetry enables real-time streaming of operational data (interface counters, BGP state, CPU/memory) from network devices to a collector. Unlike SNMP polling, telemetry is push-based and supports sub-second data granularity.
        p
          strong Enabling Telemetry
        ul
          li
            strong Topology Builder (all nodes):
            |  In Step 3 (Addressing), check
            |
            span.help-tag Enable gRPC/gNMI telemetry
            | . Configure collector IP, port, encoding, sample interval, and TLS.
          li
            strong Per-node:
            |  Select a node → Info tab → set
            |
            span.help-tag gRPC Telemetry
            |  to
            |
            span.help-code Enabled
            | . The node's startup config will include telemetry configuration.
        p
          strong Collector Settings
        ul
          li
            span.help-tag Collector IP
            |  — IP address of your Telegraf/gNMI collector (default:
            |
            span.help-code 0.0.0.0
            | )
          li
            span.help-tag Collector Port
            |  — gRPC port (default:
            |
            span.help-code 50051
            | ). Juniper uses 32767, Arista uses 6030, Nokia uses 57400.
          li
            span.help-tag Encoding
            |  —
            |
            span.help-code GPB
            |  (Protobuf, most efficient),
            |
            span.help-code JSON
            | , or
            |
            span.help-code JSON-IETF
          li
            span.help-tag Sample Interval
            |  — 5s (real-time monitoring), 10s, 30s (default), 60s (low overhead)
          li
            span.help-tag TLS
            |  — Enable TLS encryption for gRPC transport
        p
          strong Sensor Paths (OpenConfig)
        p Each vendor has default sensor paths that are automatically configured:
        ul
          li
            strong Juniper:
            |
            span.help-code /junos/system/linecard/interface/
            | ,
            span.help-code /junos/system/linecard/cpu/
            | , BGP
          li
            strong Cisco NX-OS:
            |
            span.help-code sys/intf
            | ,
            span.help-code sys/bgp
            | ,
            span.help-code sys/procsys
          li
            strong Arista:
            |
            span.help-code /interfaces/interface/state/counters
            | ,
            span.help-code /network-instances/.../bgp
          li
            strong Nokia:
            |
            span.help-code /interface[name=ethernet-*]/statistics
            | ,
            span.help-code /network-instance/.../bgp/neighbor
        p
          strong Exporting Telegraf Pipeline Config
        p From the File menu, select
          |
          span.help-tag Export Telegraf Config
          |  to download a complete
          |
          span.help-code telegraf.conf
          |  file. This includes:
        ul
          li
            span.help-code [agent]
            |  — collection interval and flush settings
          li
            span.help-code [[outputs.prometheus_client]]
            |  — Prometheus metrics endpoint on port 9273
          li
            span.help-code [[inputs.gnmi]]
            |  — per-node gNMI subscriptions with device IP, credentials, and sensor paths
        p
          strong Pipeline Setup
        ol
          li Install Telegraf:
            |
            span.help-code apt install telegraf
            |  or download from influxdata.com
          li Copy the exported config:
            |
            span.help-code cp telegraf.conf /etc/telegraf/
          li Start Telegraf:
            |
            span.help-code systemctl start telegraf
          li Install Prometheus:
            |
            span.help-code docker run -p 9090:9090 prom/prometheus
          li Add scrape target in prometheus.yml:
            |
            span.help-code targets: ['localhost:9273']
          li Install Grafana:
            |
            span.help-code docker run -p 3000:3000 grafana/grafana
          li Add Prometheus datasource → Import dashboard ID
            |
            span.help-code 12489
        p
          strong Troubleshooting Telemetry
        p Right-click any telemetry-enabled node →
          |
          span.help-tag Troubleshoot
          |  → expand the
          |
          span.help-tag 📡 Telemetry Validation
          |  section. Key commands:
        ul
          li
            strong Juniper:
            |
            span.help-code show system services extension-service request-response grpc
            | ,
            span.help-code show services analytics sensor
          li
            strong Cisco:
            |
            span.help-code show telemetry transport
            | ,
            span.help-code show telemetry control database subscriptions
          li
            strong Arista:
            |
            span.help-code show management api gnmi
          li
            strong Nokia:
            |
            span.help-code info / system grpc
        p
          strong Vendor gRPC Ports (Reference)
        table.help-table
          tr
            th Vendor
            th Default Port
            th Protocol
          tr
            td Juniper
            td 32767
            td gRPC/gNMI
          tr
            td Cisco NX-OS
            td 50051
            td gRPC/MDT
          tr
            td Cisco IOS-XR
            td 57400
            td gRPC/gNMI
          tr
            td Arista
            td 6030
            td gNMI
          tr
            td Nokia SR Linux
            td 57400
            td gNMI
          tr
            td SONiC
            td 8080
            td gNMI

    //- ── Day-0 Device Staging ────────────────
    .help-section
      button.help-section-title(type='button', (click)='toggleHelpSection("staging")')
        span.help-chevron {{ isSectionOpen('staging') ? '▾' : '▸' }}
        | Day-0 Device Staging
      .help-section-body(*ngIf='isSectionOpen("staging")')
        p
          strong What it is:
          |  Foundational config (NTP, SNMP, LLDP, Syslog, DNS, AAA users + TACACS, Banner) that gets prepended to every node's generated startup config — independent of fabric routing/protocols. Ideal for onboarding new switches or rolling out an SNMP/syslog change without touching fabric config.

        p
          strong Two scopes:
        ul
          li
            span.help-tag Fabric defaults
            |  — set once in <strong>Devices → Device Staging (Day-0)…</strong>. Applied to every node in the topology.
          li
            span.help-tag Per-node overrides
            |  — set on a node's <strong>Staging</strong> tab. Override any field; leave blank to inherit the fabric default (shown as placeholder).

        p
          strong Edit / Preview tabs in the Device Staging dialog:
        ul
          li
            strong Edit
            |  — fill in NTP servers, SNMP community/contact/location/trap targets, LLDP enabled, syslog hosts + severity, DNS servers + search domain, local users + TACACS, banners.
          li
            strong Preview &amp; Push
            |  — shows a per-vendor rendered preview (one panel per unique vendor in the topology). Multi-vendor topologies see e.g. one panel for Junos
            span.help-code set system ntp server …
            | , one for Cisco/Arista
            span.help-code ntp server …
            | , one for SONiC
            span.help-code sudo config ntp add …
            | . Unsupported vendors are flagged with an amber ⚠ chip.

        p
          strong Three ways to push staging only (without touching fabric config):
        ol
          li
            strong Per-node — node properties Staging tab
            | : "Push Staging to Device" button, with a Preview button beside it. Auto-backs up running config first.
          li
            strong Bulk — Devices → Push Day-0 Staging…
            | : scope = All / Selected / Unmapped (Day-0 onboarding sweet spot), Push or Preview-only mode, Save-to-startup toggle, sequential toggle. Live log shows
            span.help-code [Switch-1] ✓ pushed (N cmds)
            |  per device.
          li
            strong Workflow Library — "Apply Staging Only" playbook
            | : pickable from the Workflow Library; same vendor wrapping as the dialogs but runs as a workflow with audit trail.

        p
          strong Supported vendors:
          |  juniper, cisco (IOS / NX-OS / IOS-XR), arista, hpe (Comware), dell (OS10), huawei, nokia, sonic, mikrotik, extreme. Other vendors get an amber ⚠ chip in the Preview pane and are skipped at push time with a clear log line.

        p
          strong How push wraps per vendor:
        ul
          li
            strong Junos
            | :
            span.help-code configure private; set …; commit and-quit
            |  (atomic via a single
            span.help-code cli -c
            | ).
          li
            strong Cisco / Arista / Dell OS10
            | :
            span.help-code configure terminal
            |  →
            span.help-code … set lines …
            |  →
            span.help-code end
            |  →
            span.help-code write memory
            | .
          li
            strong HPE Comware / Huawei
            | :
            span.help-code system-view
            |  →
            span.help-code … lines …
            |  →
            span.help-code quit
            |  →
            span.help-code save force
            | .
          li
            strong Nokia
            | :
            span.help-code enter candidate
            |  →
            span.help-code …
            |  →
            span.help-code commit
            | .
          li
            strong SONiC
            | : raw
            span.help-code sudo config …
            |  shell commands +
            span.help-code sudo config save -y
            | .
          li
            strong Extreme XOS
            | : direct commands +
            span.help-code save configuration
            | .
          li
            strong MikroTik
            | : direct
            span.help-code /system …
            |  commands.

        p
          strong Persistence:
          |  Fabric staging is stored on
          span.help-code topology.staging
          | ; per-node overrides on
          span.help-code node.staging
          | . Both round-trip through the topology JSON. Save & regenerate (in the dialog) writes staging then re-runs config generation so the staging block appears in every node's startup config.

        p
          strong Pruning rule on save:
          |  A section is dropped only when EVERY field within it is empty. Setting just NTP timezone (no servers) is preserved; setting just DNS searchDomain (no servers) is preserved; etc. Console log
          span.help-code [device-staging] save: &#123;…&#125;
          |  prints the exact object being saved.

    //- ── Pushing Configuration ──────────────
    .help-section
      button.help-section-title(type='button', (click)='toggleHelpSection("push-config")')
        span.help-chevron {{ isSectionOpen('push-config') ? '▾' : '▸' }}
        | Pushing Configuration
      .help-section-body(*ngIf='isSectionOpen("push-config")')
        p
          strong The push pipeline:
          |  Reads
          span.help-code node.startupConfig
          | , wraps with vendor-specific preamble/postamble (from
          span.help-code services/vendor-command-map.ts
          | ), feeds the command list to the device via SSH, then validates the output for commit-failure markers.

        p
          strong Three entry points:
        ul
          li
            strong Devices menu → Push Configs to Devices
            |  — bulk full-config push to every mapped node with credentials.
          li
            strong Per-node — Config tab → "Push to Device (SSH)"
            |  — single-node full-config push.
          li
            strong Per-node — Config tab → "Push Config with Edit…"
            |  — review/edit the staged commands (insert deletes, prune lines) before pushing.

        p
          strong SSH credential resolution (in this order):
        ol
          li
            strong Node Info-tab fields
            |
            span.help-code node.sshUsername
            |  /
            span.help-code node.sshPassword
            |  — if both present, used directly.
          li
            strong Device Mapper inventory fallback
            |  — if either field is empty, the push driver looks up the inventory (saved under user-prefs key
            span.help-code device-inventory
            | ) and matches by mgmtIp first, then hostname (case-insensitive vs node label). Per-side fill: a node with a username but no password gets the password from inventory while keeping the per-node username override.
          li
            strong Skip with clear error
            |  — "no SSH credentials (set on node Info tab or in Device Mapper inventory)".
        p
          strong Verification:
          |  When fallback fires, the output annotation reads
          span.help-code (using credentials from inventory match: &lt;hostname&gt;)
          | . Console logs
          span.help-code [push] &lt;label&gt; using credentials from device inventory (matched &lt;hostname&gt;)
          | .

        p
          strong Sequential vs parallel push:
          |  Default is parallel — all devices push concurrently. Toggle "Sequential push" (Devices menu sticky preference, dry-run footer, or push confirmation dialog) to push one device at a time. Use sequential when you have many of the same vendor and hit lock contention (Junos
          span.help-code configure exclusive
          | , IOS-XR commit lock) or SSH rate limits.

        p
          strong Output validation:
          |  After SSH session closes, the driver scans the device's response for failure markers:
        ul
          li
            strong Hard markers (always fail)
            | :
            span.help-code commit failed
            | ,
            span.help-code configuration check-out failed
            | ,
            span.help-code mgd: error:
            | ,
            span.help-code configuration database is open
            | ,
            span.help-code syntax error
            | ,
            span.help-code unable to commit
            | .
          li
            strong Soft markers (fail only if no positive confirmation in same output)
            | :
            span.help-code unknown command
            | ,
            span.help-code invalid command
            | . Treated as transient noise when
            span.help-code commit complete
            |  /
            span.help-code [ok]
            |  also appears.
          li
            strong Junos extra check
            | : if the command list included
            span.help-code commit
            |  but the output didn't echo
            span.help-code commit complete
            | , the push is downgraded to ✗.

        p
          strong Auto-rollback:
          |  When ≥ 30% of nodes in a batch fail, you're prompted to roll back to the previous backup. The rollback push uses
          span.help-code suppressRollbackOffer=true
          |  to prevent recursive prompts if it partially fails.

        p
          strong Auto-backup before push:
          |  The driver calls
          span.help-code invSvc.backupConfig(nodeId, 'running', 'event')
          |  best-effort before sending the new config. Backup failures don't block the push but are logged.

        p
          strong Dry-run push:
          |  Devices menu → Dry Run Push (or workflow option). Renders the per-node command list into a summary dialog without sending. Footer toggle for sequential mode + a "Push to N" button to commit for real.

    //- ── Automation Suite ───────────────────
    .help-section
      button.help-section-title(type='button', (click)='toggleHelpSection("automation")')
        span.help-chevron {{ isSectionOpen('automation') ? '▾' : '▸' }}
        | Automation Suite
      .help-section-body(*ngIf='isSectionOpen("automation")')
        p Automation menu surfaces the orchestration tools. They share a common workflow execution engine (
          span.help-code services/workflow.service.ts
          | ) and target nodes selected on the canvas or all mapped nodes.

        p
          strong Workflow Library — Automation → Workflow Library
        ul
          li Pre-built parameterized playbooks: Interface ops (shut/no-shut/MTU/description/clear-counters), VLAN ops (add/remove/trunk), BGP (clear/show), maintenance (commit-save, clear-arp-host, backup-now, approval-gate, notify-webhook, apply-staging-only), diagnostics (ping, show-version, show-interfaces, fortinet-system-status).
          li Three views: Browse (categorized list) → Configure (parameters + target nodes) → Run (per-node progress + results).
          li Vendor coverage: juniper, cisco, arista, huawei, hpe, dell, sonic, nokia, mikrotik, extreme, fortinet (where applicable).
          li Each playbook synthesizes a Workflow with WorkflowSteps (
            span.help-code run_command
            | ,
            span.help-code backup_config
            | ,
            span.help-code log
            | ,
            span.help-code webhook
            | ,
            span.help-code approval
            | ) which the engine executes per target.

        p
          strong Approval Gates
        ul
          li Any workflow step can be
            span.help-code action: 'approval'
            |  — execution pauses; the gate appears in
            strong Automation → Pending Approvals
            | .
          li Each approval has: message, gate name, optional timeout in minutes (auto-rejects on expiry, 0 = wait forever).
          li Operator clicks Approve or Reject; the workflow resumes (or stops) on the next step.
          li Approval History — last 50 resolved gates with who-resolved-when, kept in memory per session.
          li
            strong Closing the app rejects all pending approvals
            |  cleanly — no leaked promises.

        p
          strong Scheduler — Automation → Scheduled Jobs
        ul
          li Recurring jobs: backup_all, poll_all, compliance_check, custom_command. Cron-style (minute, hour, day-of-week granularity).
          li Jitter (5–15 s random gap) avoids same-minute thundering herd when multiple jobs land on the same tick.
          li Click "Run" on any job row to fire it manually right now (independent of schedule).
          li Initialised once per app via a static
            span.help-code _schedulerInitialized
            |  guard so multiple tabs don't overwrite each other's job dispatcher.

        p
          strong Bulk Port Operations — Automation → Bulk Port Ops
          |  Dialog targets multiple ports across multiple nodes in one sweep. Operations: shut / no-shut / MTU / description / clear-counters / VLAN access / VLAN trunk. Vendor-aware command generation; preview before commit.

        p
          strong Outbound Webhooks
        ul
          li Workflow step
            span.help-code action: 'webhook'
            |  posts JSON to any URL — Slack, Teams, custom listener.
          li Default Slack-compatible payload shape:
            span.help-code &#123;text: "&lt;message&gt;"&#125;
            |  (extend per playbook).
          li
            span.help-code continueOnError: true
            |  by default — webhook failures don't fail the workflow.

        p
          strong Set Protocol — Devices → Set Protocol…
        ul
          li Bulk-assign a routing protocol across all or selected nodes after dropping a design template. Options: eBGP Clos (unique ASN per role), iBGP-RR (shared ASN), OSPF, OSPFv3, IS-IS, or None (clear).
          li Protocol-specific fields shown conditionally: ASN start values for eBGP, area for OSPF, level (L1/L2/L1-2) for IS-IS.
          li Reuses the role-based ASN logic from the topology builder (spine vs leaf vs border-leaf).

        p
          strong Config Diff Viewer — node Config tab → "Fetch &amp; Compare Running"
        ul
          li Pulls the running config off the device via SSH, runs an LCS-based unified diff against the local startup config.
          li Inline diff shown right in the panel; "⛶ Expand" button opens a full-window stand-alone view.
          li Stats line:
            span.help-code + N added · - M removed · = K matching
            |  in the diff header.

        p
          strong Compliance — Automation → Compliance
